  {
   "title": "Shredding Good Manners",
   "logline": "A jazz guitarist whose devotion to a jazz-metal fusion sound alienates his bandmates and drains his savings must decide whether his reputation or his music will survive a series of genteel humiliations.",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "index": 2,
     "text": "After the Blue Lantern's patrons walked out mid-solo, Mike hauled a distortion pedal onto his pawned-off amp and vowed fusion anyway."
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "text": "Against his manager's polite advice, Mike rehearsed alone in a borrowed garage, convinced that thrash riffs and bebop chords would one day be fashionable."
    },
    {
     "index": 4,
     "text": "A wedding planner booked Mike's trio for Saturday, paying only in exposure, one lukewarm sandwich, and a pointed request for Sinatra."
    },
    {
     "index": 5,
     "text": "Mike pawned his Gibson for rent, and over bitter tea with old bandmates conceded thrash and bebop were not on speaking terms."
    },
    {
     "index": 6,
     "text": "At a stiff dinner party, Mike's attempt at a metal riff ended with a shattered wine glass and a hostess's icy suggestion he leave."
    },
    {
     "index": 7,
     "text": "A tabloid critic praised the scandalously loud fusion, though Mike's old bandmates still refused to share the stage."
    }
