  {
   "title": "Strings Against the Dead End",
   "logline": "A stubborn jazz guitarist who scorns the metal fusion he once imagined chases a magical rise in fortune while losing the bandmates and money that once held him steady.",
   "sentences": [
    {
     "index": 2,
     "text": "Mike scoffed at the metal fusion idea as noise, yet his bandmates trusted him to hold the groove while his amp sat pawned."
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "text": "At the Lantern Club Mike's solo made paper moths circle the bar, but the band drifted off, leaving him with his dead-end metal blend."
    },
    {
     "index": 4,
     "text": "A record label offered Mike a modest contract, and the drummer invited him back, though Mike still called metal fusion a mistake."
    },
    {
     "index": 5,
     "text": "A studio paid Mike handsomely for session work, yet he still refused the metal idea while bandmates tolerated him warily."
    },
    {
     "index": 6,
     "text": "Mike's solo drew a sold-out hall to tears, golden light spilling from his strings, while former bandmates shunned him as a sellout."
    },
    {
     "index": 7,
     "text": "Mike played on a rooftop while city lamps bloomed into singing birds, then pawned his last guitar to pay rent."
    }
